 open at 8:15.","We leave at sunrise."]},{"title":"On and in","explanation":"Use on with days and dates, including a named day plus a part of that day. Use in with months and with the morning, afternoon or evening.","examples":["on Thursday afternoon","in November"]},{"title":"No preposition","explanation":"Usually omit a time preposition before next, last, this or every, and before today, tomorrow and yesterday. Here, — means leave the gap empty.","examples":["The rehearsal is next week.","I will call tomorrow."]}],"tips":["Read the full context before choosing.","Check the explanation, including after a correct answer.","A passing practice session is useful feedback, not a language certificate."],"source":"seed","provenance":{"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","authorship":"Grammacho; generated offline with AI assistance","kind":"original-guidance-aligned","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending","referenceIds":["bc-time","pcc-esol"],"assessment":"Practice only; levels are approximate teaching labels, not validated CEFR assessment."}}'::jsonb j) payload
